SRC1='<div class="src"><b>Sources:</b> NSW Economics Stage 6 Syllabus (Topic 3: External stability). Australian Bureau of Statistics, <i>Balance of Payments and International Investment Position, Australia</i>, June quarter 2026 (released September 2026) and earlier issues. Reserve Bank of Australia, Statistical Table I1. WA Treasury Corporation, <i>Balance of Payments Q4 2025</i>. Figures were correct in November 2026; check the ABS for the latest quarter before quoting them in an exam.</div>'
ext = '''
<div class="box"><b class="t">The big idea</b>External stability means Australia can keep meeting its financial obligations to the rest of the world over the medium to long term. It is judged by whether the current account deficit, foreign liabilities and exchange rate are on a sustainable path, not by any single quarter's number.</div>
<h2>1. How external stability is measured</h2>
<table><tr><th style="width:27%">Syllabus measure</th><th style="width:38%">What it tells you</th><th>Latest evidence</th></tr>
<tr><td>Current account deficit (CAD) as a % of GDP</td><td>How much more Australia pays the world than it receives in a year, for trade, income and transfers, relative to the size of the economy.</td><td>Deficit of $27.2b in the June quarter 2026. About 3.3% of GDP over the year to June 2026.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Net foreign debt as a % of GDP</td><td>What Australians have borrowed from overseas minus what they have lent. Debt must be serviced with interest whatever happens to profits.</td><td>About $1,481b at December 2025, a record in dollar terms.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Net foreign liabilities as a % of GDP</td><td>Net foreign debt plus net foreign equity. The broadest measure of what Australia owes the world.</td><td>$638.9b at 30 June 2026. This is smaller than net debt because Australians own more equity overseas than foreigners own here.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Terms of trade</td><td>Export prices relative to import prices (export price index &divide; import price index &times; 100). A rise means a given volume of exports pays for more imports.</td><td>Well below the 2022 peak. Higher fuel prices lifted the import bill in 2026.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exchange rate</td><td>The value of the Australian dollar, against the US dollar or the Trade Weighted Index. Sharp falls can signal a loss of confidence and raise the cost of servicing foreign-currency debt.</td><td>Use the Economy at a Glance tool for today's rate.</td></tr>
<tr><td>International competitiveness</td><td>Whether Australian producers can win sales at home and abroad, through price (costs, productivity, the dollar) and non-price factors (quality, reliability).</td><td>Weak productivity growth is the main long-run concern.</td></tr></table>
<h2>2. Trends to know</h2>
<ul><li><b>Long history of deficits.</b> Australia ran a CAD in almost every year from the 1970s to 2019, usually between 3% and 6% of GDP.</li>
<li><b>A rare run of surpluses.</b> Record commodity prices produced current account surpluses from 2019 to 2023, the first sustained surpluses in about half a century.</li>
<li><b>Back to deficit.</b> As commodity prices eased and the income deficit widened, the CAD returned. In 2026 even the balance on goods and services moved into deficit (&minus;$5.1b in the June quarter) as fuel and vehicle imports rose.</li>
<li><b>The income deficit is the persistent part.</b> The net primary income deficit was $21.9b in the June quarter 2026, most of the CAD. It reflects interest and dividends paid on decades of foreign investment in Australia.</li></ul>
<h2>3. Causes and effects</h2>
<table><tr><th style="width:50%">Causes</th><th>Effects</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Structural:</b> national saving is lower than national investment (the saving and investment gap), so the gap is filled with foreign capital; a narrow export base concentrated in commodities; high net primary income payments on past borrowing and foreign ownership.<br><b>Cyclical:</b> falls in the terms of trade; slower growth in trading partners; strong domestic growth pulling in imports; a high dollar reducing competitiveness.</td>
<td><b>Positive:</b> foreign capital lets Australia invest more than it saves, lifting productive capacity, growth and jobs. If borrowing funds investment that earns more than it costs, the debt pays for itself (the "consenting adults" or Pitchford view).<br><b>Negative:</b> growing servicing costs widen the income deficit (the debt trap scenario); exposure to shifts in global investor sentiment; exchange rate volatility; possible credit rating downgrades and higher interest rates; a constraint on how fast the economy can grow.</td></tr></table>
<div class="act"><h3>Activity 1: Calculate the measures <span class="mk">5 marks</span></h3><p>A hypothetical economy has GDP of $2,800b. Its current account deficit is $84b, net foreign debt is $1,400b and net foreign liabilities are $700b. Its export price index rose from 100 to 120 last year while its import price index stayed at 100. This year the export price index is 114 and the import price index is 105.</p>
<p>(a) Calculate the CAD, net foreign debt and net foreign liabilities as a percentage of GDP. (b) Calculate the terms of trade for last year and this year, and the percentage change. Show working.</p>'''+lines(6)+'''</div>
<div class="act"><h3>Activity 2: Read the real data <span class="mk">6 marks</span></h3><p>Use the table in section 1 and the trends in section 2.</p>
<p>(a) Net foreign liabilities ($638.9b) are much smaller than net foreign debt (about $1,481b). Explain what this tells you about Australia's net foreign equity position.</p>'''+lines(3)+'''
<p>(b) Explain why the net primary income deficit is described as the "structural" part of the CAD.</p>'''+lines(3)+'''
<p>(c) Give one reason the balance on goods and services moved into deficit in 2026.</p>'''+lines(2)+'''</div>
<div class="act"><h3>Activity 3: Build the chain</h3><p>Complete each cause and effect chain with at least three links. Use the Chain Builder tool to check your logic.</p>
<p>(a) A fall in iron ore prices &rarr; ........................ &rarr; ........................ &rarr; a larger CAD.</p>
<p>(b) Low national saving &rarr; ........................ &rarr; ........................ &rarr; a larger net primary income deficit.</p>
<p>(c) A depreciation of the Australian dollar &rarr; ........................ &rarr; ........................ &rarr; an effect on the CAD over time.</p></div>
<div class="act"><h3>Activity 4: Does the CAD matter? <span class="mk">discussion</span></h3><p>Sort these statements into "the CAD is not a problem" and "the CAD is a concern", then write a two-sentence judgement of your own.</p>
<ul><li>Most of Australia's foreign debt is owed by private firms and banks, not the government.</li><li>Servicing costs take a growing share of export income.</li><li>The floating dollar depreciates when confidence falls, which improves competitiveness.</li><li>Borrowing has funded mining and infrastructure investment that raised export capacity.</li><li>A sudden stop in capital inflow would force higher interest rates.</li></ul>'''+lines(3)+'''</div>
<div class="act"><h3>Activity 5: HSC-style questions</h3><p>(a) Outline TWO measures of external stability. <span class="mk">2 marks</span></p><p>(b) Explain TWO causes of a current account deficit in Australia. <span class="mk">4 marks</span></p><p>(c) Analyse the effects of a fall in the terms of trade on Australia's external stability. <span class="mk">6 marks</span></p><p>Answer on your own paper. Find past questions on this dot point in the Question Finder.</p></div>'''+SRC1
doc('12Eco External Stability Notes and Activities', O, 'HSC Economics &middot; Topic 3 Economic Issues', 'External stability: notes and activities', 'Measurement, trends, causes and effects', ext)

SRC2='<div class="src"><b>Sources:</b> NSW Economics Stage 6 Syllabus (Topic 4: Labour market policies). Fair Work Commission, <i>Annual Wage Review 2026</i> decision, 2 June 2026, and published summaries of the decision. Fair Work Act 2009 (Cth). Fair Work Ombudsman. Figures were correct in November 2026.</div>'
lab = '''
<div class="box"><b class="t">The big idea</b>Labour market policies set the rules for how wages and conditions are decided and try to improve the skills and job-readiness of the workforce. They are microeconomic policies: their aim is a more productive, flexible and fair labour market.</div>
<h2>1. The national and state systems</h2>
<ul><li>The <b>national system</b> operates under the <i>Fair Work Act 2009</i>. It covers most private-sector employees in Australia.</li>
<li>Every state except Western Australia has handed its private-sector industrial relations powers to the Commonwealth. <b>State systems</b> still cover most state public servants and, in several states, local government workers. Western Australia keeps a state system for unincorporated businesses.</li>
<li>Two national bodies matter most: the <b>Fair Work Commission (FWC)</b>, the independent tribunal that sets minimum wages, makes awards, approves agreements and resolves disputes; and the <b>Fair Work Ombudsman</b>, which gives advice and enforces the law.</li></ul>
<h2>2. How the national system determines pay and conditions</h2>
<table><tr><th style="width:26%">Syllabus dot point</th><th>How it works</th><th style="width:17%">Method</th></tr>
<tr><td>Minimum employment standards</td><td>The National Employment Standards are legal minimums for every national-system employee. Examples: maximum ordinary hours of 38 a week, four weeks of annual leave, parental leave, notice of termination and redundancy pay. No award or agreement can undercut them.</td><td>Centralised</td></tr>
<tr><td>Minimum wages</td><td>An FWC expert panel runs the Annual Wage Review. In June 2026 it lifted the national minimum wage to <b>$26.44 an hour ($1,004.90 a week)</b> from 1 July 2026, a rise of about 6%, and raised award minimum wages by <b>4.75%</b>. Close to three million workers were affected.</td><td>Centralised</td></tr>
<tr><td>Awards</td><td>Modern awards set minimum pay rates and conditions for an industry or occupation: classifications, penalty rates, overtime, allowances. They are the safety net that agreements are tested against.</td><td>Centralised</td></tr>
<tr><td>Enterprise agreements</td><td>Negotiated between an employer and its employees, usually through a union, then approved by the FWC. Employees must be better off overall than under the award. Since 2022 bargaining across several employers has been easier.</td><td>Decentralised</td></tr>
<tr><td>Employment contracts for high income earners</td><td>Employees earning above the high income threshold (about $183,100 in 2025&ndash;26, indexed each July) can be given a guarantee of annual earnings, after which award terms need not apply. Pay is settled between the individual and the employer.</td><td>Individualised</td></tr></table>
<h2>3. Dispute resolution</h2>
<p>Most disputes are settled at the workplace using the procedure every award and agreement must contain. If that fails, the FWC can <b>conciliate</b> or <b>mediate</b> (help the parties agree) and, in some cases, <b>arbitrate</b> (impose a binding decision). Employees can take <b>protected industrial action</b> only while bargaining for a new agreement and after a ballot. Breaches of the law are pursued by the Fair Work Ombudsman or through the courts.</p>
<h2>4. Centralised, decentralised or individualised?</h2>
<table><tr><th style="width:18%">Method</th><th>Arguments for</th><th>Arguments against</th></tr>
<tr><td>Centralised (tribunal decisions)</td><td>Protects low-paid workers with little bargaining power; limits wage inequality; lets wage growth be managed across the economy.</td><td>Wages not linked to each firm's productivity; rigid across industries and regions; large across-the-board rises can add to inflation or unemployment.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Decentralised (enterprise bargaining)</td><td>Links pay to productivity at the workplace; allows conditions to suit the firm; rewards efficiency.</td><td>Workers in weak bargaining positions fall behind; wider wage dispersion; bargaining takes time and can cause disputes.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Individualised (individual contracts)</td><td>Maximum flexibility; rewards individual skill and performance.</td><td>The employer usually holds more power; least protection; may widen inequality, including the gender pay gap.</td></tr></table>
<h2>5. Education, training and employment programs</h2>
<p>These policies work on the supply side of the labour market. They aim to cut structural and long-term unemployment, lift participation and raise productivity. Examples include employment services that match job seekers to vacancies (Workforce Australia), fee-free TAFE places and apprenticeship incentives in areas of skill shortage, and Jobs and Skills Australia, which advises on future workforce needs. They take years to work and are hard to evaluate.</p>
<div class="act"><h3>Activity 1: Who decides? <span class="mk">5 marks</span></h3><p>For each worker, name the instrument that most likely sets their pay and say whether it is centralised, decentralised or individualised.</p>
<table><tr><th>Worker</th><th style="width:30%">Instrument</th><th style="width:22%">Method</th></tr>
<tr><td>A casual retail assistant at a small independent shop</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>A nurse in a large private hospital with a union-negotiated deal</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>A senior engineer earning $240,000 a year</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>A cleaner with no award or agreement covering the job</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>A teacher in a NSW government school</td><td></td><td></td></tr></table></div>
<div class="act"><h3>Activity 2: The 2026 wage decision <span class="mk">6 marks</span></h3><p>Unions asked for a larger rise than the Commission granted and employer groups asked for a smaller one.</p>
<p>(a) The national minimum wage was $24.95 an hour before 1 July 2026. Show that the new rate of $26.44 is a rise of about 6%.</p>'''+lines(2)+'''
<p>(b) Explain one argument a union would make for a large rise and one argument an employer group would make for a small one.</p>'''+lines(4)+'''
<p>(c) Explain how a rise in minimum wages above the rate of inflation could affect aggregate demand and the costs of firms.</p>'''+lines(3)+'''</div>
<div class="act"><h3>Activity 3: Weigh it up</h3><p>"Decentralised wage determination is better for the Australian economy than centralised wage determination." Write one paragraph that agrees and one that disagrees. Use at least two terms from: productivity, inflation, inequality, flexibility, bargaining power.</p>'''+lines(8)+'''</div>
<div class="act"><h3>Activity 4: HSC-style questions</h3><p>(a) Distinguish between an award and an enterprise agreement. <span class="mk">2 marks</span></p><p>(b) Explain how education and training programs can reduce structural unemployment. <span class="mk">4 marks</span></p><p>(c) Discuss the effects of a more decentralised system of wage determination on economic growth and the distribution of income. <span class="mk">6 marks</span></p><p>Answer on your own paper. Use the Extended Response Builder to plan part (c).</p></div>'''+SRC2
doc('12Eco Labour Market Policies Notes and Activities', P, 'HSC Economics &middot; Topic 4 Economic Policies and Management', 'Labour market policies: notes and activities', 'The national system, wage determination, dispute resolution and training', lab)

SRC3='<div class="src"><b>Sources:</b> NSW Economics Stage 6 Syllabus (Topic 4: Limitations of economic policies; Policy responses and their effects). Reserve Bank of Australia, <i>The Transmission of Monetary Policy</i> explainer. Australian Bureau of Statistics, Balance of Payments, March and June quarters 2026. WA Treasury Corporation, <i>Balance of Payments Q1 2026</i>.</div>'
lim = '''
<div class="box"><b class="t">The big idea</b>No policy works perfectly or instantly. The syllabus names three limits on what governments and the Reserve Bank can achieve: time lags, global influences and political constraints. Strong extended responses use these limits as part of the argument, not as a list at the end.</div>
<h2>1. Time lags</h2>
<table><tr><th style="width:20%">Lag</th><th>What it is</th><th style="width:34%">Example</th></tr>
<tr><td>Recognition</td><td>Time taken to see that a problem exists. Data arrive late and are revised.</td><td>GDP figures are published about two months after the quarter ends.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Implementation (decision)</td><td>Time taken to decide on a response and put it in place.</td><td>Budget measures need drafting and often legislation. The RBA board meets eight times a year and can act at any meeting.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Impact (effect)</td><td>Time taken for the policy to change spending, output, jobs and prices.</td><td>The RBA estimates the full effect of a rate change takes one to two years.</td></tr></table>
<table><tr><th style="width:22%">Policy</th><th>Implementation lag</th><th>Impact lag</th></tr>
<tr><td>Monetary</td><td>Short. A decision takes effect the next day.</td><td>Long. It works through borrowing, saving, asset prices and the exchange rate.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fiscal</td><td>Medium to long. It follows the annual Budget cycle and Parliament. Automatic stabilisers have no decision lag.</td><td>Shorter for payments to households; longer for infrastructure.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Microeconomic</td><td>Long. Reform needs reviews, consultation and legislation, often with the states.</td><td>Very long. Gains in productivity appear over many years, while costs to some groups appear at once.</td></tr></table>
<p><b>Why it matters:</b> by the time a policy bites, conditions may have changed. A stimulus that arrives after the recovery has begun adds to inflation, so policy can become pro-cyclical by mistake.</p>
<h2>2. Global influences</h2>
<ul><li><b>The international business cycle.</b> Growth in trading partners, especially China, drives demand for Australian exports whatever domestic policy does.</li>
<li><b>Commodity prices and the terms of trade.</b> These swing national income, company tax receipts and the Budget balance.</li>
<li><b>Global interest rates and capital flows.</b> If overseas rates rise while Australia's do not, capital flows out and the dollar falls, which adds to imported inflation.</li>
<li><b>Supply shocks.</b> In 2026 conflict in the Middle East pushed up fuel prices. Australia's current account deficit widened to $27.1b in the March quarter and $27.2b in the June quarter, and the RBA could do little about the cause.</li>
<li><b>International agreements.</b> Trade and climate commitments narrow the choices open to governments.</li></ul>
<h2>3. Political constraints</h2>
<ul><li><b>The electoral cycle.</b> With federal elections about every three years, governments favour policies with quick, visible benefits and delay unpopular ones such as tax reform or spending cuts.</li>
<li><b>Parliament.</b> A government without a Senate majority must negotiate, so measures are amended, delayed or dropped.</li>
<li><b>Interest groups.</b> The costs of reform fall on small, organised groups while the benefits are spread thinly, so opposition is louder than support.</li>
<li><b>Federalism.</b> Many reforms in health, education, housing and energy need the states to agree.</li>
<li><b>Independence of the RBA.</b> This removes monetary policy from the electoral cycle, which is one reason it carries most of the load in managing demand.</li></ul>
<div class="box"><b class="t">Linking it together</b>These limits are the reason for a <b>policy mix</b>. Monetary policy is quick to change but blunt. Fiscal policy can be targeted but is slow and political. Microeconomic policy lifts long-run capacity but takes years. Objectives can also conflict: lower unemployment can mean higher inflation, and faster growth can widen the CAD.</div>
<div class="act"><h3>Activity 1: Name the limitation <span class="mk">6 marks</span></h3><p>Label each scenario as a time lag (and which one), a global influence or a political constraint. Some fit more than one: justify your choice.</p>
<table><tr><th>Scenario</th><th style="width:34%">Limitation and reason</th></tr>
<tr><td>A government shelves a plan to broaden the GST nine months before an election.</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>The RBA raises the cash rate, but household spending keeps growing for another year.</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>A fall in iron ore prices cuts company tax receipts and pushes the Budget into a larger deficit.</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Unemployment data show the labour market had already weakened three months before anyone acted.</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>A national skills reform stalls because two states will not sign the funding agreement.</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Overseas central banks lift interest rates and the Australian dollar depreciates.</td><td></td></tr></table></div>
<div class="act"><h3>Activity 2: Draw the lags</h3><p>Draw a timeline from "economy begins to slow" to "policy has its full effect" for (a) a cut in the cash rate and (b) a new infrastructure program. Mark the recognition, implementation and impact lags on each, and write one sentence comparing them.</p>'''+lines(7)+'''</div>
<div class="act"><h3>Activity 3: Apply it to 2026 <span class="mk">6 marks</span></h3><p>In 2026 higher world fuel prices raised Australia's import bill and added to inflation.</p>
<p>(a) Explain why this is a global influence on the effectiveness of monetary policy.</p>'''+lines(3)+'''
<p>(b) Explain one fiscal response the government could make and one political constraint on it.</p>'''+lines(4)+'''</div>
<div class="act"><h3>Activity 4: HSC-style questions</h3><p>(a) Define the term "impact lag". <span class="mk">1 mark</span></p><p>(b) Explain how time lags can reduce the effectiveness of fiscal policy. <span class="mk">4 marks</span></p><p>(c) Extended response plan: "Evaluate the effectiveness of macroeconomic policies in achieving the government's economic objectives." Write a plan with four paragraphs. At least two must use a limitation as evidence for your judgement. <span class="mk">plan only</span></p><p>Use the Policy Simulator to see lags at work, and the Extended Response Builder to write the plan.</p></div>'''+SRC3
doc('12Eco Limitations of Economic Policies Notes and Activities', P, 'HSC Economics &middot; Topic 4 Economic Policies and Management', 'Limitations of economic policies: notes and activities', 'Time lags, global influences and political constraints', lim)

ans = '''
<div class="box"><b class="t">Teacher copy</b>Suggested answers for the three notes-and-activities handouts. Written as a marking aid, not as model answers. Check the figures against the latest ABS and Fair Work releases before use.</div>
<h2>External stability</h2>
<p><b>Activity 1.</b> (a) CAD 84 &divide; 2,800 = 3.0% of GDP. Net foreign debt 1,400 &divide; 2,800 = 50%. Net foreign liabilities 700 &divide; 2,800 = 25%. (b) Last year 120 &divide; 100 &times; 100 = 120. This year 114 &divide; 105 &times; 100 = 108.6. Change (108.6 &minus; 120) &divide; 120 = a fall of 9.5%.</p>
<p><b>Activity 2.</b> (a) Net foreign liabilities = net foreign debt + net foreign equity. Because liabilities are smaller than debt, net equity is negative as a liability: Australians hold more equity abroad than foreigners hold in Australia (a net foreign equity asset position of roughly $840b on these figures). (b) It reflects the accumulated stock of past borrowing and foreign ownership, so it must be paid every period whatever happens to trade; it does not reverse with the cycle. (c) Higher fuel prices and stronger vehicle imports raised goods debits; lower commodity prices than at the peak reduced export receipts.</p>
<p><b>Activity 3.</b> (a) Lower export prices &rarr; lower terms of trade &rarr; lower export revenue and a smaller balance on goods and services &rarr; larger CAD. (b) Saving below investment &rarr; reliance on foreign capital inflow &rarr; larger foreign liabilities &rarr; more interest and dividends paid abroad. (c) Exports cheaper and imports dearer &rarr; export volumes rise and import volumes fall over time (after an initial J-curve worsening) &rarr; improved balance on goods and services; valuation effect raises the dollar value of foreign-currency debt.</p>
<p><b>Activity 4.</b> Not a problem: private debt; floating dollar; borrowing funded investment. Concern: servicing costs; sudden stop. Reward a reasoned judgement either way.</p>
<p><b>Activity 5.</b> (a) Any two syllabus measures with a brief description. (b) One structural and/or one cyclical cause, each explained with a link to the current account. (c) Should trace lower export income to the balance on goods and services and the CAD, then to the exchange rate, national income, foreign liabilities and servicing, and recognise offsetting effects of depreciation.</p>
<h2>Labour market policies</h2>
<p><b>Activity 1.</b> Retail assistant: modern award, centralised. Nurse: enterprise agreement, decentralised. Engineer: individual contract with a guarantee of annual earnings above the high income threshold, individualised. Cleaner: national minimum wage and the National Employment Standards, centralised. Teacher: state system (NSW Industrial Relations Commission award), centralised; the point is that this worker is outside the national system.</p>
<p><b>Activity 2.</b> (a) (26.44 &minus; 24.95) &divide; 24.95 = 0.0597, about 6%. (b) Union: restore real wages lost to inflation, living costs of the low paid, reduce inequality and the gender pay gap. Employer: higher labour costs, risk to jobs and hours in small business, weak productivity growth, risk of adding to inflation. (c) Higher real incomes for low-paid workers with a high propensity to consume raise consumption and aggregate demand; higher unit labour costs unless matched by productivity, which may be passed on in prices or met by cutting hours.</p>
<p><b>Activity 3.</b> Agree: wages linked to productivity, flexibility, lower inflation risk. Disagree: bargaining power, inequality, slower wage growth for weaker groups.</p>
<p><b>Activity 4.</b> (a) An award is an industry or occupation safety net made by the FWC; an agreement is negotiated at the enterprise and must leave employees better off overall than the award. (b) Should link skills mismatch to structural unemployment and explain how training improves occupational mobility and employability. (c) Growth: productivity and flexibility against possible weaker consumption. Distribution: wider dispersion, effect on low-paid and award-reliant workers.</p>
<h2>Limitations of economic policies</h2>
<p><b>Activity 1.</b> GST: political constraint (electoral cycle). Cash rate: impact lag. Iron ore: global influence. Unemployment data: recognition lag. Skills reform: political constraint (federalism); also implementation lag. Overseas rates: global influence.</p>
<p><b>Activity 2.</b> Cash rate: short recognition and implementation, long impact (one to two years). Infrastructure: long implementation (planning, approval, legislation) and a long, drawn-out impact; risk it arrives when the economy has already recovered.</p>
<p><b>Activity 3.</b> (a) The shock is cost-push and imported. Raising rates cannot increase fuel supply; it can only reduce demand, at a cost to growth and jobs, so the RBA faces a trade-off it cannot remove. (b) For example temporary cost-of-living relief or a fuel excise cut. Constraints: cost to the Budget, pressure not to remove relief once given, need for legislation, risk of adding to demand and inflation.</p>
<p><b>Activity 4.</b> (a) The time between a policy being implemented and its full effect on the economy. (b) Should explain at least two lags with reference to the Budget cycle and the risk of pro-cyclical policy. (c) Reward plans where limitations support the judgement and where the policy mix is recognised.</p>'''
doc('12Eco Suggested Answers External Stability Labour Market Limitations (Teacher)', '#1c3fb0', 'HSC Economics &middot; Teacher copy', 'Suggested answers', 'External stability, labour market policies and limitations of economic policies', ans)
